00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.250
So let's begin with, what
is Google Cloud Dataproc?

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.639
Dataproc is Google Cloud
Platform's managed Hadoop

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:11.990
and Spark service.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.810
Its overarching goal
is ultimately just

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.930
to help tip the scales
away from having

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.280
to think about Hadoop as
low level infrastructure.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.260
So to help illustrate
a little bit

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.780
of what it means
to be fast and easy

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.480
and cost effective,
imagine, if you will,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.090
the next version of
Hadoop or Spark comes out.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.460
The shiny new version
promises something

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.075
like a 10x speed up of all
your workloads or, say,

00:02:37.075 --> 00:02:39.280
you can simplify one of
your 10 stage pipelines

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.660
into a one liner in Spark.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:46.085
So just consider, what might
it take to go from that time

00:02:46.085 --> 00:02:47.710
where you've heard
about these versions

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.850
and want to try it out to
being able to deploy this,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.380
to canary this and be ready
to switch over your production

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.170
pipelines to the latest and
greatest at full production

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.770
scale.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.280
Well, with Dataproc, it should
be no more than a one liner

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.890
on the command line,
or alternatively, just

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.660
about four clicks on
the Cloud Console.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.460
And about 90 seconds
later you should

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.720
be up and running with
the latest and greatest,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.060
ready to canary your workloads
even at full production scale.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.700
And this same kind of
speed and simplicity

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.000
is also what lends itself
to cost savings, ultimately,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.640
because you can more easily
ensure that you're only

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.550
deploying the resources that
you need when you need them.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:32.250
So right off the bat, you can
significantly reduce your cost

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.770
just by making sure that
you're actually paying

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.000
for what you actually use.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.480
To put this in perspective
in the big picture,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.310
ultimately there
is a whole spectrum

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.670
of approaches to running Hadoop
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.550
And this ranges from
thinking about GCP

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.360
as your infrastructure
to thinking

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.700
about GCP as a platform.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.670
Now along this whole
spectrum, Google

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.430
works with third
party Hadoop vendors

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.140
and provides open source
connector libraries and tools.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.780
So in general, your
migration doesn't

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.529
have to be an all or
nothing proposition.

00:04:09.529 --> 00:04:11.820
Now some of the concepts that
we're talking about today

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.640
are going to be applicable no
matter what kind of approach

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.296
to Hadoop on GCP you
take, while others

00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:18.420
are going to be highlighting
new architectures that

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.930
are made possible by moving
to this fully managed

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:25.340
Dataproc side of the spectrum.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.590
Taking a peek under the hood
at how Dataproc is made,

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:35.070
Dataproc layers in features
in between the layers

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.460
of the underlying Hadoop
stack, and adds these features

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.720
for easy visibility into
status and monitoring,

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.920
adds control hooks and
configuration hooks

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.870
for things like
managing your clusters,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.660
resizing your clusters,
optimizations.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.630
But it does so on top
of the open source

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.420
interfaces of the Hadoop
stack, ultimately.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.300
So what this means
is Dataproc adheres

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.920
to the principle
of letting you drop

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.090
down one level into the lower
level stack whenever you need.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.960
So you don't have to abandon
any of your low level,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.310
advanced customizations
if you don't want to just

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.270
to get the value added
features of Dataproc.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.270
You can bring all that with you
and still keep those low level

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.900
customizations.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.960
So as an example here, we have
Dataproc clients and raw Hadoop

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.460
clients.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.860
So if you want, you
can interact entirely

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.510
with your Dataproc
clusters using

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.220
the standard underlying raw
Hadoop clients, connecting over

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.980
to TCP.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.080
And if you don't want
to, you never even

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.570
have to acknowledge the
existence of Dataproc's Jobs

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:36.071
API.

00:05:36.071 --> 00:05:37.486
On the other side
of spectrum, you

00:05:37.486 --> 00:05:39.800
might choose to only
interact with your clusters

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.330
through Dataproc Jobs APIs,
through these standardized

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.210
web-based REST
interfaces, and never

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:51.290
even have to be the
wiser about VMs existing

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.890
under the hood at all.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.990
But both of these
options are available.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:58.820
Dataproc's Hadoop
distribution is

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:03.230
based on the open source
Apache Big Top project,

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.040
and natively will
install and support

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.910
Pig, Hive, Hadoop, and Spark.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:13.100
Other ecosystem tools like
Tez, Zeppelin, and Kafka

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.340
can all be activated from the
Dataproc distribution using

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.040
something called initialization
actions, which are just scripts

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.220
that you can use to customize
your Dataproc clusters

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.030
for your own needs.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:29.880
So that brings us to planning
a proof of concept, or a POC.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.220
Thinking back to that
on-premise static cluster,

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.680
you may find yourself becoming
increasingly dependent

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.700
on increasingly monolithic
Hadoop clusters.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.160
And in a lot of ways,
some of the key strengths

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.460
of Hadoop as a
great data platform

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.350
are also the source
of its weaknesses.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.610
And what I mean by
that is there are

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.780
all these components
in the Hadoop ecosystem

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.760
that make it so
easy, they're so well

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.740
integrated with the
rest of the Hadoop stack

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.800
that it's easy to augment
your existing Hadoop

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.880
cluster with powerful tools
like Oozie for scheduling

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.430
or Zeppelin or Jupiter
for data science, maybe

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.380
various HiveServer2 clients.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.366
And along the way, you
don't really notice,

00:07:15.366 --> 00:07:16.740
but then one day
you'll look back

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.440
and you'll see
that somehow you've

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.590
dumped all this systemic
complexity in one

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:26.820
place on your cluster and
loaded it down in such a way

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:31.950
that it's that much more
difficult, in aggregate, to now

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.320
reshape or rearchitect
your workloads when

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.900
it becomes necessary to do so.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.850
So what this means
for your POC is,

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:42.000
as you're thinking
about how to migrate,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.730
you want to make sure you're not
just thinking about forklifting

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.490
your entire Hadoop
stack onto GCP,

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:52.830
complexity and messy
hacks included.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.830
But instead you want
to think about keeping

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.710
your eyes open for opportunities
to simplify your architecture

00:07:58.710 --> 00:07:59.280
as you go.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:08.440
This is something that, as
you keep in mind that clusters

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:13.510
aren't an end unto themselves,
that no single cluster should

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:14.260
really--

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.290
you don't want single clusters
to constitute your entire data

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:20.510
analytics universe in a
sealed, hermetic way anymore.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:23.360
You think about the broader
platform on the whole.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.210
So for example,
you'll use clusters

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:29.200
as building blocks towards your
greater data platform at large.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.250
And perhaps you'll use BigQuery
for the bulk of your data

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:34.449
warehousing needs.

00:08:34.449 --> 00:08:36.490
Maybe you'll find that
Stackdriver is the easiest

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.739
way to unify your logging and
monitoring across all your GCP

00:08:39.739 --> 00:08:40.239
services.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:50.070
Now as one of the first
steps towards your POC

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.290
and subsequent
migration, you'll want

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.914
to make sure that you
outline your high level

00:08:54.914 --> 00:08:57.330
goals, the driving forces of
what you're trying to get out

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.010
of your migration to GCP.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.070
And typically these are going to
be high level goals that can be

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:06.250
achieved in any number of ways.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.174
So for example, if you're
thinking about cost savings,

00:09:09.174 --> 00:09:11.340
you'll want to make sure
you're thinking about price

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.590
performance and total
cost of ownership

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.450
instead of getting stuck
on comparing CPU for CPU

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:19.010
or byte for byte storage.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:20.870
Another example in
terms of scaling

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:24.550
elasticity-- if you're
unlocking this elasticity,

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:28.910
GCP isn't just a way to speed
up the hardware requisitioning

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:29.880
cycle.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:30.560
It can be.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.810
It can be a faster way
to requisition hardware

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:33.890
in a traditional way.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.220
But really, you get the
most mileage out of larger

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.870
architectural changes.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.809
So for example, by separating
out your storage and compute

00:09:41.809 --> 00:09:43.850
and scaling them independently,
or separating out

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.700
different workloads and
scaling them independently,

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:53.150
this is how you can achieve
the best scalability for what

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:56.930
you've paid for doing this
migration in terms of making

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.670
changes to your architecture.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:01.790
Now these are just
some examples of goals

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.940
that you might come in
with and how GCP features

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:05.840
might relate to them.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.210
Ultimately you know best
what your driving goals are.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:12.245
And this is how you will shape
your overall migration plan.

00:10:12.245 --> 00:10:14.370
Now it's probably also
worth mentioning right now--

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.457
I know I've been talking
a lot about change,

00:10:16.457 --> 00:10:18.290
change this, change the
way you're thinking,

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.580
change your architecture.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:22.670
And admittedly it's scary
to talk about change.

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.280
Change comes with
cost in itself.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.710
It can be a source of
complexity in itself.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:31.120
And it's definitely
a valid concern

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.710
to think about the
cost of change.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:38.720
But what's important is both you
should neither write off change

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.750
altogether, nor should you
blindly accept change as good.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.400
So it can be good,
it can be bad.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:45.707
But it doesn't have to
be a significant cost.

00:10:45.707 --> 00:10:47.540
And there are definitely
opportunities here,

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.580
during your Hadoop
migration especially,

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:53.120
for low hanging fruit where
the cost isn't that significant

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.680
and you have significant
benefits for the long term

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.679
viability of your architecture.

00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:01.970
So as long as you're weighing
those costs and benefits,

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.053
that's what's important,
even if it doesn't always

00:11:04.053 --> 00:11:07.190
land on the side of choosing
that big architecture change,

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:10.730
as long as you know what's going
into that decision to do so.

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:23.160
On a more concrete level,
you'll want to identify as much

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.310
of the characteristics that
go into the requirements

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:28.230
of your long term
architecture as possible.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.180
So really characterize
your existing stack

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:31.860
and figure out
what you're doing.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.879
And I focus on the
characteristics

00:11:34.879 --> 00:11:37.170
less so than the requirements
because ultimately you're

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:41.220
not just coming up with a
checklist of requirements.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.400
By characterizing your stack,
this is a great opportunity

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.070
to identify what
components you really love

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:50.002
and which ones are really
providing you value long term

00:11:50.002 --> 00:11:51.960
and will end up in your
long term architecture,

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.820
while you'll find
that a lot of things

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.930
are really just workarounds or
a means to an end, that actually

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.931
bring you pain but were a
necessary evil in the meantime,

00:12:00.931 --> 00:12:03.180
and something that you can
really slice out complexity

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.310
with by doing your
cloud migration

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.360
and adopting something
a little more cloud

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.930
native for those pieces.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:13.830
On a more practical level,
the logistics of planning out

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:15.741
your timeline and
capacity planning,

00:12:15.741 --> 00:12:18.240
that's something that you'll
want to do early on as possible

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:19.380
in your workflow.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.165
So coming up with your estimates
of what you'll need over

00:12:22.165 --> 00:12:24.540
the course of your migration
to make sure that you're not

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:27.960
suddenly blocked by
something silly like a quota

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.360
request when it comes
time to actually ramp up

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:31.050
your migration.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.070
So that brings us
to data migration.

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:40.370
This is one of the first
concrete technical items

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.370
that you will be tackling
during the course of your POC

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.080
and migration.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:47.750
The cornerstone of
all your migration

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.840
is likely to be the Google Cloud
Storage connector for Hadoop.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:56.240
So this is a connector that
Google provides and implements

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.490
the Hadoop file
system interfaces

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.038
on top of Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.720
So we strive really for
out-of-the-box compatibility.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.050
So this means that,
for the most part,

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:11.630
your migration will take
place using familiar Hadoop

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.420
tools that you're already
using inside your Hadoop stack,

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:18.620
running simple
DistCp jobs into GCS.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.500
Other options that we
provide include HDFS

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.322
on persistent disk, which
comes standard with Dataproc.

00:13:25.322 --> 00:13:26.780
Or alternatively
you might consider

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.870
using local SSD as a higher
performance alternative.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.554
So good for things
that require--

00:13:32.554 --> 00:13:34.220
where you might want
to pay a little bit

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:38.660
extra in order to guarantee
very low round trip latency

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.930
for things like business
intelligence or online data

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:42.590
analytics.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:48.640
Slightly larger
architectural change

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.820
involves potentially replacing
your entire HBase cluster

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:53.650
with Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:58.150
So in the same vein as GCS
being a file system replacement,

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.820
Google provides HBase compatible
clients for Cloud Bigtable

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.510
so that you can transparently
migrate over any workloads that

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.570
normally run against
HBase and use Google Cloud

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:09.230
Bigtable instead and treat
it like an HBase cluster.

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:11.110
And this is something
that will drastically

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:12.670
reduce your
operational complexity

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:16.090
by no longer having to manage
your HBase cluster yourself.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:19.090
Another option is using BigQuery
for your data warehousing

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:20.880
and SQL based analytics.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:25.410
So in the same vein we provide
connectors for BigQuery,

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:27.580
for Hadoop, so
that you can still

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.596
interact with your
storage in BigQuery

00:14:29.596 --> 00:14:30.970
while doing the
bulk of your data

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:34.060
analytics in BigQuery
itself, with zero ops,

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.660
without managing your clusters.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:42.910
Timeline-wise, some of the data
storage plans that you take on

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:46.180
will help shape your
migration strategy in itself.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.040
So as an example,
one recurring pattern

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:52.210
is you might find that you're
archiving data into GCS.

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:55.162
Or you might find
that regardless of how

00:14:55.162 --> 00:14:56.620
you want to migrate
your workloads,

00:14:56.620 --> 00:15:02.080
you're starting to do disaster
recovery backup into GCS.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:03.880
So in these cases,
it's very natural

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:07.910
to start adopting ad hoc use
cases inside of Dataproc.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.850
So now that your data
is already there,

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:14.320
you might find that an
unexpected backfill comes up.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.810
And you can use Dataproc
to get that done quickly.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.700
Or maybe new
pipeline development

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:21.610
where you don't want to
occupy space on-premise,

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:26.990
it's a natural fit for
using ad hoc Dataproc usage.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:30.080
Digging a little bit deeper
into the compatibility of GCS

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.820
and HDFS, like I mentioned,
the GCS connector

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:40.460
is designed to be compatible for
the vast majority of Hadoop use

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:42.080
cases, so a drop in replacement.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.870
Anything that uses file
input/output formats,

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:47.480
Spark text, binary files,
all these things just

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:50.720
work without you having
to think about it.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:53.120
That said, there are
some caveats, right,

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:55.760
because Google Cloud Storage
is an object store, not

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:56.910
a file system.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.970
And this is something
that's becoming also

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:03.170
increasingly common within
the Hadoop ecosystem itself

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:04.600
is to use these object stores.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:08.810
So different cloud providers
and private cloud solutions

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:13.070
like OpenStack Swift also have
similar object store semantics.

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:17.090
And this is something that's
recognized and becoming

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:19.640
better supported within
the Hadoop community itself

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:20.180
day to day.

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:21.900
So that will get better.

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:26.300
But in the meantime,
it means that there

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:31.760
are some more specialized
HDFS features that

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:33.620
might require still using HDFS.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:35.522
This includes
directory semantics.

00:16:35.522 --> 00:16:36.980
So an object store
doesn't actually

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.750
have directories, so
you don't have things

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:41.580
like directory access times.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.590
Additionally, objects
are immutable once they

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:44.150
are created.

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:48.770
So one use case that might still
do well on HDFS instead of GCS

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:54.140
would be if you have a workload
that requires appendable files.

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:57.380
In general though, a
lot of these constraints

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:02.040
come from architectural designs
that are actually benefits.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.390
So for example, some of
this increased round trip

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:08.810
latency comes as a result
of no longer having

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.119
any single point of failure
or any single bottleneck.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:15.200
So you can have arbitrarily many
files in a single GCS bucket

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:18.619
if you want, a single GCS
directory, because it's all

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:21.170
massively distributed on
Google infrastructure, right?

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:26.900
And as an implementation
detail, that

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:28.790
means there's an extra
layer of indirection,

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:34.340
which is why latency might take
a little bit longer round trip.

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:38.900
In general, the same principles
for optimizing your HDFS

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.740
workloads applies
to GCS as well.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:47.980
So using small files can hurt
in terms of round trip latency.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:50.250
So avoiding lots of
small reads, avoiding

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:54.600
lots of sequential small
metadata requests, using bulk

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.540
reads of data whenever possible
all still equally apply to GCS.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:09.200
So to visualize what it might
look like to actually run

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:12.680
this data transfer from your
on-premise cluster into GCS,

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:14.660
there are generally
two categories.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:17.180
You can have push-based
or pull-based transfer

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:18.355
of your data.

00:18:18.355 --> 00:18:19.730
The most
straightforward way here

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:23.300
is push-based where you simply
install the GCS connector

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:26.780
on-premise and use
the same public APIs

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:28.691
to access GCS as
any other client.

00:18:28.691 --> 00:18:30.440
So this is a very
straightforward approach

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.481
as long as you can modify
your on-premise cluster

00:18:32.481 --> 00:18:36.280
and you have some spare capacity
for running, say, DistCp

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:37.310
MapReduce jobs.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:39.020
And this will just
push everything

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.300
over your public
internet gateway

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:42.980
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:47.190
And in-transit,
GCS requires HTTPS.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:51.320
So everything is protected
by that SSL layer.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:54.110
As an alternative,
if you can't do that,

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.030
you can set up a
Cloud VPN gateway

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:59.010
to bridge your on-premise
network with a Compute Engine

00:18:59.010 --> 00:18:59.510
network.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.760
So Dataproc supports
deploying your clusters

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:05.510
into custom subnetworks
that have all the IP

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:09.410
address and firewall rules that
you need to make your VPN work.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:13.040
So once you've
bridged your networks,

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:17.480
Dataproc cluster is
being built on raw,

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:20.720
native, compatible Hadoop
clients can, out of the box,

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.310
interact with your
on-premise cluster

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:24.660
and pull data out
through that VPN tunnel.

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:28.370
So you simply specify
your HDFS colon slash

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:31.700
slash on-premise name node path.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.220
And everything just works.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:37.340
And one benefit of running
Dataproc clusters like this

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.440
to pull the data
out of on-premise

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:43.250
is that you can use
Dataproc's cluster sizing

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:48.380
features in order to adjust
your transfer rate as needed to.

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:51.570
And this also doesn't have
to be putting data into GCS.

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:55.300
You might be performing these
transfers into HDFS on Dataproc

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:55.800
instead.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.700
So that brings us to some
overarching cloud architecture

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:02.200
concepts.

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.580
Like I mentioned before, there's
no one size fits all answer.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:11.280
So again, these are just
some building blocks

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:15.000
that you can assemble into
your own migration strategy.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.850
But these are recurring
patterns that are well utilized

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:19.840
in a variety of places.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.500
So to begin with, I
want to think back again

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.630
to that on-premise cluster.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.490
And traditionally,
Hadoop places a lot

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:33.055
of emphasis on data
locality and bin packing

00:20:33.055 --> 00:20:33.930
and things like this.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:36.360
And it makes a lot
of sense in that it's

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.600
common to get to come in
and provision a cluster--

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.370
and plan out a Hadoop cluster
based on storage size.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.079
So you'll say I need like
an x petabyte cluster,

00:20:45.079 --> 00:20:46.620
and that's a certain
number of disks.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.500
And you can adjust your
CPU and RAM a little bit.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:52.110
But in large part, you're
still driven by saying,

00:20:52.110 --> 00:20:54.360
you need X petabytes of storage.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.540
And you just have to
plan to then make do

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.510
with what compute comes
with it, for the most part.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:03.760
And ultimately, this
is a leaky abstraction.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:08.580
This is letting storage
implementation details start

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:11.910
to dictate application
layer decisions of how

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:15.270
your workloads are shaped and
how your workloads are sized.

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:17.580
So on Google Cloud
Platform, you don't have

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.080
to worry about bin packing.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.150
That's Google's
problem to worry about.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:25.770
And instead, you can separate
out your storage and compute.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:29.400
And not only do you get your
cost savings by only having

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.650
to pay for what you're
actually using and having

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:34.119
independent scaling,
this actually

00:21:34.119 --> 00:21:35.910
also simplifies your
workflows, because you

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:39.302
don't have to
worry about shaping

00:21:39.302 --> 00:21:41.760
your workloads to the right
size for your hardware anymore.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.510
But rather you do it
the other way around,

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:47.730
pick the right hardware
for your workflows.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:50.070
So this is a concept
that actually

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.796
unlocks a lot of other
architectural concepts as well.

00:21:53.796 --> 00:21:55.170
And to put it in
terms of number,

00:21:55.170 --> 00:22:01.440
imagine if you have a job that
takes say, 10,000 vCPU hours.

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:05.100
On cloud, it costs the same
whether you provisioned 20,000

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:07.500
vCPUs for half an
hour, or if you

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:11.880
provision 100 CPUs
for 100 hours,

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.260
if the math works out-- yes.

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:17.590
So in that case,
it costs the same,

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:21.504
but the data is so much
more valuable to you

00:22:21.504 --> 00:22:22.420
now rather than later.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:25.600
So you don't have to try to
slip it into some spare capacity

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:29.075
and let it run between the
cracks of some existing

00:22:29.075 --> 00:22:29.700
infrastructure.

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:32.320
You just burst into the
cloud and get the job

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:35.650
done immediately so that you
can have the data as quickly

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.300
and as valuably as possible.

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:44.170
One nice feature of Compute
Engine that Dataproc natively

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.460
supports is preemptively VMs.

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:49.880
So preemptively VMs are
just like any other VM.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.590
They operate just
like any other VM.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:53.930
The only difference is that
Google reserves the right

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:58.250
to reclaim these
VMs, to reclaim them,

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:02.380
preempt them if other on
demand or production workloads

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:03.980
make it necessary.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:08.060
And as a result, they only cost
20% of the normal cost of a VM,

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.950
so a significant cost savings.

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:14.210
And normally it might
be difficult to manage

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.070
these kinds of things,
joining and leaving

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:17.111
your cluster by yourself.

00:23:17.111 --> 00:23:20.390
But on Dataproc,
that's Dataproc's job

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:25.762
to make sure that it replenishes
these VMs if they leave

00:23:25.762 --> 00:23:28.220
the cluster and make sure that
they smoothly join and leave

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:31.479
the cluster over the
course of your workload.

00:23:31.479 --> 00:23:33.770
So that means that it's
basically just fire and forget.

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:35.840
You set your number
of preemptible VMs

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.450
on your data cluster,
let the job run,

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:41.180
and you get better
price performance.

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:44.200
Another Compute Engine feature
that Dataproc natively supports

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:49.430
is custom VM types, which Urs
talked about in today's keynote

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:50.210
as well.

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.582
These, again, go
back to the notion

00:23:52.582 --> 00:23:54.290
that you don't have
to worry bin packing.

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:55.730
That's Google's problem.

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:58.460
So you don't have to worry about
stranding resources because

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:00.950
of an oddly sized workload
or having to underprovision

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.260
for a given workload, right?

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.480
Pick exactly the right
mix of CPU and RAM

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.200
that you need, and
Dataproc will work it out.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:11.620
Dataproc will dynamically
calculate the right Hadoop

00:24:11.620 --> 00:24:15.410
and HDFS configs based on
the actual requested amount

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:16.650
of CPU and RAM.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:27.010
One of the bigger
architectural concepts

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:30.430
that is enabled by that
disaggregation of storage

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:31.186
and compute--

00:24:31.186 --> 00:24:32.560
and I mentioned
this a little bit

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:33.976
before too, is
that you don't want

00:24:33.976 --> 00:24:36.724
to think about single clusters
as your entire universe.

00:24:36.724 --> 00:24:38.140
You don't want
your Hadoop cluster

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:41.860
to be your hermetically sealed
data analytics universe.

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.170
Instead, the broader
platform as a whole

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:46.460
is your data analytics platform.

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:50.920
And so in this concept,
you'll separate out

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.620
different workloads
into different clusters.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.090
That way this
eliminates a lot of

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:58.900
this multiplicative complexity
where you try to bin pack

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:01.360
a lot of different things
into the same place.

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.630
So you can significantly
simplify things that way.

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:07.060
And what's more, you can
independently optimize these.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:10.810
So for example, you might
find that some workloads,

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:12.460
like say your
business intelligence

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:16.330
or your online
analytics, prefers

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:20.170
paying a little bit more for
very high performance and very

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:22.960
predictable performance.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.110
So you might opt for
something like HDFS on SSD

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:28.870
and cache a lot of
your data assets

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:32.440
inside of that HDFS
for fast performance.

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:35.170
And on the other hand, you
might have some overnight ETL

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:38.500
pipelines, batch jobs, that you
just want to optimize for cost.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:42.070
So in that case, they can be
completely differently shaped.

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:44.470
You might mix in a
lot of preemptible VMs

00:25:44.470 --> 00:25:46.930
to sacrifice a little bit
of that predictability

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:51.440
of variable job run time in
order to get much better cost

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:51.940
savings.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:57.890
In the same vein, not only
can you chop up clusters

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:00.890
based on the shape
of your workloads,

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:03.470
but also chop them up in
terms of your lifecycle.

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:07.290
So what this means is on
the far end of the spectrum,

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:09.680
if you have ephemeral
clusters, this really

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.360
gets rid of a lot of
the headaches that

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:15.110
come from dealing
with high complexity,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.380
long lived clusters,
so single use clusters

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:22.220
that are made possible by
Google's minute granularity

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:24.470
billing and fast
deployment time.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:28.820
And this makes it trivially easy
to ensure that your hardware is

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:32.390
able to evolve in lockstep
with the evolution

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.860
of your workloads.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:36.350
This also means things
like even migrating

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:38.600
to a whole different region
are as trivial as flipping

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:44.090
one configuration value in
your ongoing ephemeral cluster

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.680
workload.

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:47.960
In the middle
ground, there's also

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.740
this notion of using
semi-long lived clusters.

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:53.450
So in this case,
instead of using

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.080
a single very large and
forever lived cluster,

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:59.030
chop it up into a small
handful of semi-long

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:02.630
lived clusters where you can
still preserve much of the look

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:05.510
and feel of an
always on cluster.

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:07.760
You always have clusters
available there.

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:11.600
But instead, you're sort
of load balancing your jobs

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:15.230
into individual clusters
inside a group, transparently.

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:18.194
And by doing so, what you can
do is, for example, if you want

00:27:18.194 --> 00:27:19.610
to canary a brand
new version, you

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:23.840
get a nice hermetic environment
for performing this A/B

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:24.420
testing.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:28.400
You can canary one entire
cluster unto itself

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.679
without any risk of
leaking over and breaking

00:27:30.679 --> 00:27:31.970
your other production clusters.

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:34.430
But you can canary that
with a brand new version,

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:37.720
naturally let some amount
of your production workloads

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:38.540
go to that cluster.

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:41.510
And if it's all green, it's
very easy to now upgrade.

00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:45.830
You're not trying to change
the tires on a moving vehicle

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:46.580
anymore.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:50.190
You get to do this in chunks
one cluster at a time.

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:52.940
Similarly, you don't have
scaling bottlenecks anymore,

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:55.280
or single points of
failure, because now you

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.710
can keep adding more
clusters to your group

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.170
in order to increase your
scale without worrying

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:02.270
about the single
bottleneck of scaling.

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:09.997
So that brings us to managing
your clusters and jobs.

00:28:09.997 --> 00:28:11.830
So I want to take a
look at what it actually

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:14.590
looks, what it actually feels
like to manage your clusters

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.660
and jobs on Dataproc.

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:21.160
And I have slides
here for posterity.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:25.150
But we will do this
live in demo so you

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:28.010
can get a better feel of
what it actually looks like.

00:28:28.010 --> 00:28:31.450
So I mentioned before, a one
liner on the command line.

00:28:31.450 --> 00:28:34.030
And this one is a
little bit more complex.

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:37.824
The basic command
that you'll run is--

00:28:37.824 --> 00:28:41.830
whoops-- gcloud dataproc
clusters create.

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:43.500
Give it a cluster name.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.790
And actually you can hit
Enter right then if you want.

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:48.940
In my case, I'm going to
call this dhuo-demo-1.

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:50.520
I'm going to give
it five workers.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.200
I'm also going to
turn on this property

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.880
for enabling
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:56.800
So you can see how we can
build some custom dashboards.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.443
Also I'm going to attach a tag.

00:28:58.443 --> 00:29:00.660
You'll see how that
plays into everything.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:03.720
And importantly I've also
put a time command here.

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:07.370
So you can keep me honest.

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:10.140
We can see how long this cluster
actually takes to deploy.

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:13.180
So these are all clients
that are based on Dataproc's

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.180
web-based REST interfaces.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:18.180
That means that you get
ubiquitous and consistent

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:20.670
access from everywhere.

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:22.890
Excitingly, one of
my colleagues is also

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:24.960
concurrently using
this demo project

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:28.230
to do another live
demo next door.

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:34.140
And while that is doubly
tempting for the demo fates,

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:39.009
no reason to be worried
precisely because we're

00:29:39.009 --> 00:29:40.050
using different clusters.

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:42.300
It doesn't matter if we're
sharing the same resources.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:43.260
It's completely safe.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:44.720
We can do anything we want.

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:46.480
We won't break each other.

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.304
As I mentioned, so
kicked of that cluster

00:29:49.304 --> 00:29:50.220
from the command line.

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:52.824
And sure enough, it's deploying
in this web interface.

00:29:52.824 --> 00:29:54.240
So these are
different clients all

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.760
talking to the same
REST interface.

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:59.370
And it also means that
third party vendors

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:04.140
are able to integrate with
these same open source REST APIs

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:05.500
using auto generated libraries.

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:07.290
So we have native
support in things

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:11.190
like Apache Airflow, in
Luigi, in Python mrjob.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:13.830
So in all these open
source projects,

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:16.140
you can get Dataproc
support out of the box.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:19.200
Also show what it looks like
to create a cluster entirely

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:21.740
from the UI.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:24.660
You just select the dropdown,
give it a name, and hit Enter.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:25.980
It's that easy.

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.180
So there, I created
another cluster.

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:31.380
In fact, let's create
another one, for fun.

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:33.300
In this case, I
mentioned you might

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:36.750
want to use the latest and
greatest versions of Hadoop.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:38.850
And what might that entail?

00:30:38.850 --> 00:30:42.940
Well we always provide
a preview image version,

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:45.750
which has the latest and
greatest software that's

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:48.420
going to be targeted
for the next release.

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:51.150
So right now Hadoop 2.8 hasn't
actually been released yet.

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:53.070
But we build again
snapshots of Hadoop 2.8.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:54.990
So we can select
this preview image.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:56.850
And in those four clicks--

00:30:56.850 --> 00:30:58.320
I think that was four clicks--

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:00.870
we will have a
Hadoop 2.8 snapshot.

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.210
Not official Hadoop 2.8,
but a snapshot cluster

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:08.140
that lets us be ready
for that next release.

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:10.177
So going back to
here, our time command

00:31:10.177 --> 00:31:12.010
says that took one
minute and eight seconds,

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:14.250
so actually less
than 90 seconds.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.575
In 68 seconds we've
now deployed a cluster,

00:31:16.575 --> 00:31:17.450
and it's ready to go.

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:20.060
The second that it says that
it's done, it is indeed ready.

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:23.410
And I'm going to go ahead
and run an SSH tunnel.

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:28.700
So it sounds tricky, but it's
really just another one liner.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:32.920
I'm going to do dynamic port
forwarding on port 12345.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:35.200
-N, so it's not
taking standard input.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:39.570
And I'm going to start
up a new browser,

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:40.930
point it at that cluster.

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:44.040
And let's see, I have
to turn this off.

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:46.080
So sure enough, this
cluster's ready.

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:48.440
This is your standard
familiar Yarn UI.

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:51.120
So we're not locking you out
of any of your familiar Hadoop

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:53.490
idioms by adding Dataproc.

00:31:53.490 --> 00:31:54.980
So we have our five nodes.

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:57.240
This cluster is ready to go.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:31:59.910
I mentioned being able to
submit from raw client, so let's

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:01.880
go ahead and SSH
into that, demo-1.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:09.960
So on this cluster,
I have access

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:11.400
to all my low level clients.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:15.475
Let's go ahead and run hadoop
jar lib/hadoop-mapreduce.

00:32:18.592 --> 00:32:21.420
It's examples.jar.

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:24.590
And we can teragen some data.

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:26.480
And like I mentioned
before also,

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:28.400
drop in replacement for HDFS.

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:33.740
Instead of specifying HDFS path,
I just say gs:// and everything

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:35.970
just works magically.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:38.570
teragen, let's call it tmp1.

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:41.027
So this is not using
Dataproc's Job API.

00:32:41.027 --> 00:32:42.860
So this is just using
the raw Hadoop client.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:45.230
And here's your
familiar driver output.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.740
It will tell you the status of
your MapReduce job as it goes

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:49.810
and everything.

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:52.160
And sure enough,
as this is running,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:53.480
we'll see it pop up in Yarn.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:55.130
So we can see the
status of this.

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:58.100
And in this case, there's
no application tags.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.550
This was one raw, not
through Dataproc's Jobs API.

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:05.780
But at least we can see,
it just works against GCS

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:11.690
without any extra
fancy configuration.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:15.570
Alternatively, you can use
Dataproc's gcloud command.

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:17.375
So jobs submit.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:23.340
Specify your cluster.

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:24.900
Specify your JAR file.

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:28.000
And I just have the Hadoop
MapReduce example's JAR local

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:28.500
here.

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.340
I'll just open it
and [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:30.340 --> 00:33:33.910
So [INAUDIBLE] that JAR.

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:36.370
Standard Hadoop JAR file,
it's all compatible.

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.102
I don't have to
recompile it to run

00:33:38.102 --> 00:33:39.310
on Dataproc or anything that.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:41.380
Pull it out of
any Hadoop distro.

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:43.270
And I have this local.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:46.100
In this terminal, I'm not
SSHed into the cluster.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:49.226
And if I want, I can completely
seal off this cluster

00:33:49.226 --> 00:33:50.350
from the outside world too.

00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:52.330
We recently just
launched the ability

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:55.000
to deploy private
IP only clusters.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.730
So you can create clusters
that don't have any public IP

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:01.240
addresses, and you can still
interact with it through all

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:03.280
your Dataproc Jobs API.

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:06.440
And it's just full access
to all GCP services.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:08.080
So let's go ahead and run this.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:10.413
And I'm intentionally running
this without any arguments

00:34:10.413 --> 00:34:11.590
here just so you can see.

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:14.620
There's no SSH session
here, but we funnel out

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:15.790
the driver outputs.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.440
So I made a mistake.

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:19.460
I didn't give any arguments.

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:22.530
And so the job will fail and
print out this help text.

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:25.760
It will tell me, oh, I need
to specify an argument.

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:30.600
So even though I'm not SSHed
in, but Dataproc's client, job

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:32.770
submission client
preserves the look and feel

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:34.750
of interacting
with these drivers

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:37.810
so that you can up arrow and
you can have a fast development

00:34:37.810 --> 00:34:42.190
loop to react to any errors
that are coming from your job.

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:48.989
So let's go ahead and
also specify teragen

00:34:48.989 --> 00:34:50.860
demo/teragen_temp2.

00:34:57.810 --> 00:35:02.180
In this case, we are submitting
through the Dataproc Jobs API.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:03.430
So let's see.

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:04.960
I want to show
these side by side,

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:08.020
but my screen's
a little smaller.

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:10.450
In any case, this
look and feel again

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:12.676
preserves what it
feels like to run

00:35:12.676 --> 00:35:14.050
from your standard
Hadoop client.

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:16.960
So if you have something that
likes to stash away the driver

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:20.227
output as you're running,
it's easy to switch over

00:35:20.227 --> 00:35:22.060
to the Dataproc jobs
client without changing

00:35:22.060 --> 00:35:23.230
the way you think about it.

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:24.760
And I just did Control-C there.

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:27.835
Normally I'd be out of luck if
I did Control-C after running

00:35:27.835 --> 00:35:28.840
a Hadoop JAR.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:36.430
But with Dataproc jobs, you
can rejoin waiting for any job.

00:35:36.430 --> 00:35:37.390
So I Control-C it.

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:39.160
But I just have to
say wait for that job,

00:35:39.160 --> 00:35:42.040
and it just rejoins that driver
stream right where I left off.

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:43.630
I could have thrown
away this laptop,

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:46.720
opened up a brand new
laptop, and continued

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:48.070
without skipping a beat.

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:50.180
And this also means
that that job--

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:53.950
so here was that job that
we intentionally failed.

00:35:53.950 --> 00:35:56.020
It gave us help text.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:58.900
And here is that job that
succeeded, that we just ran.

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:01.780
So ubiquitous access from
different job clients.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:05.005
You can see the job output here.

00:36:05.005 --> 00:36:06.130
So that makes it very easy.

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:09.210
You can check in on your jobs
with your phone if you want.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:10.270
It's very convenient.

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:14.340
Now, let's see, so going over
this Stackdriver monitoring.

00:36:14.340 --> 00:36:16.080
We also enabled
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:17.760
And one concept
I talked about is

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:21.150
using this to provide
overarching monitoring

00:36:21.150 --> 00:36:24.472
of multiple different Dataproc
clusters in one place.

00:36:24.472 --> 00:36:25.680
So let's create a group here.

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:28.350
We tagged our cluster with--

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:32.680
I believe it was dhuo-live-demo.

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:36.190
So let's call it
dhuo-live-demo here.

00:36:36.190 --> 00:36:38.440
And you can filter it by tag.

00:36:42.410 --> 00:36:44.710
So let's just say that
if the tag exists,

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:46.945
this constitutes a group.

00:36:46.945 --> 00:36:49.070
So this way I can manage
multiple Dataproc clusters

00:36:49.070 --> 00:36:52.852
with the same tag and put them
in the same monitoring group.

00:36:52.852 --> 00:36:55.310
So let's see what it takes to
create a brand new monitoring

00:36:55.310 --> 00:36:58.040
dashboard from scratch and
see if we can do it live.

00:36:58.040 --> 00:36:59.270
So I just created a group.

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:03.140
Now all I have to do is go in,
create a brand new dashboard.

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:03.910
It's untitled.

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:09.350
I'll call it dhuo demo, and
start dragging and dropping

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:11.720
to add these charts.

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:17.050
We'll see, there are built in
host side monitoring values.

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:18.891
And so let's see,
network traffic,

00:37:18.891 --> 00:37:19.890
let's make it aggregate.

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:21.540
So we use the stack area.

00:37:21.540 --> 00:37:24.580
And we can filter by group.

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:29.150
So let's choose our
dhuo-live-demo group.

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:30.550
Great, so now we have a chart.

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:32.550
Data's going to start coming in.

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:34.140
Let's add another chart.

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:36.930
We'll add a couple of these.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:42.020
Let's see, outbound
is good, filter.

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:42.920
So it's that easy.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:47.250
All I have to do,
point and click,

00:37:47.250 --> 00:37:49.930
and I have myself a nice
monitoring dashboard.

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:51.582
Aside from using
Stackdriver in itself,

00:37:51.582 --> 00:37:53.790
you can also just click
through to your cluster-- oh,

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:55.887
that was the wrong cluster--

00:37:55.887 --> 00:37:57.970
click through to your
cluster, and there's already

00:37:57.970 --> 00:37:59.219
some amount of built-in stuff.

00:37:59.219 --> 00:38:01.780
So this was our
teragen job running.

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:06.100
So you can see CPU utilization,
network bytes, and it pops up.

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:08.350
So that we have something
more interesting to monitor,

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:11.100
let's kick off something
a little bit more beefy.

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:14.080
So a bit of a throwback here.

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:16.540
For old times sake, let's
sort of bunch of data.

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:22.690
So I think I have
some data here.

00:38:22.690 --> 00:38:27.330
And let's put the
output in terasort.

00:38:30.620 --> 00:38:32.950
So let me submit
this job, and we're

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:34.660
going to sort a
bunch of data and see

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:36.880
what monitoring looks like.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:39.020
We can also take a peek at logs.

00:38:39.020 --> 00:38:42.100
So we're well integrated
with Stackdriver Logging.

00:38:42.100 --> 00:38:45.260
And what you can
do here is, going

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:48.260
through cloud Dataproc cluster,
you can search for dhou-demo-1.

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:53.130
And sure enough, we
have one cluster there.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:56.470
And as that job kicks off, we
have these different categories

00:38:56.470 --> 00:38:56.970
of logs.

00:38:56.970 --> 00:38:59.910
We have daemon logs, so you
can see about the health

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:01.220
of your overall cluster.

00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:02.370
There's also user logs.

00:39:02.370 --> 00:39:05.222
So user logs will be associated
with each of the jobs.

00:39:05.222 --> 00:39:06.930
And this is all funneled
into Stackdriver

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.700
so that you can search here.

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:11.130
You can actually create
log-based metrics too.

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:13.860
So that's something that
we recently released

00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:16.500
as well with a bit of a
refactor of our logging.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:18.630
So you can set up
monitoring charts and alerts

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:21.850
based on what kind of
logs are coming through.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:26.810
So here we have the job
that we just started,

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:27.810
that we just kicked off.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.440
So you can see how
it's kicking off

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:32.430
a lot of individual map tasks,
all your usual stuff usually.

00:39:32.430 --> 00:39:33.888
You don't have to
dig through these

00:39:33.888 --> 00:39:36.090
unless something goes wrong.

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:39.120
Now taking a look, this is
kind of taking a long time.

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:40.050
I'm impatient.

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:44.020
It took like a minute to get
a third of the way through.

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:45.420
So what does that mean?

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:48.681
Is that 30 minutes
to complete this job?

00:39:48.681 --> 00:39:50.430
And yeah, we don't
have that kind of time.

00:39:50.430 --> 00:39:52.144
So, we're impatient.

00:39:52.144 --> 00:39:53.310
We want to make it run fast.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:55.440
What can we do?

00:39:55.440 --> 00:40:00.960
What if we make this thing have
20 times as much resources.

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:02.490
How hard is it to scale that up?

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:08.049
Well, that was
about four clicks.

00:40:08.049 --> 00:40:08.590
I lost count.

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:12.760
But about four clicks later,
we resized our cluster up

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.610
to a hundred nodes instead
of just five nodes.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:17.140
It's not quite done yet.

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:19.210
It's not 100% instantaneous.

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:20.950
But the second we
clicked that, we saw

00:40:20.950 --> 00:40:23.650
these GCE VMs start to pop up.

00:40:23.650 --> 00:40:26.644
So you see these
green checked boxes.

00:40:26.644 --> 00:40:27.810
They're starting to come in.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:30.018
They still have a little
bit of initialization to do.

00:40:30.018 --> 00:40:31.750
But as they come in,
we're going to see

00:40:31.750 --> 00:40:33.350
that we have this running job.

00:40:33.350 --> 00:40:35.558
And in Yarn we're going to
see this active node count

00:40:35.558 --> 00:40:36.270
start going up.

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:39.310
So even in the middle of a
job, we felt that it was slow.

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:42.580
So we can immediately do a
couple of clicks in the console

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:45.202
and immediately start
seeing new nodes come in.

00:40:45.202 --> 00:40:46.660
Well we'll take
another look there.

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:47.790
We're also going to--

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:51.390
let's go ahead and add a
few other monitoring charts.

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:56.570
So I use some host
side monitoring.

00:40:56.570 --> 00:40:58.580
What we did, I clustered
deployment time,

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:01.940
was we install the Stackdriver
agent, which reports guest,

00:41:01.940 --> 00:41:04.270
reported utilization metrics.

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:07.250
So this means we can
even look at fork rate--

00:41:07.250 --> 00:41:09.440
sure, why not?

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:14.390
We can look at CPU,
according to the guest,

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:18.004
so that you can see if there's
any kind of discrepancy.

00:41:18.004 --> 00:41:19.670
And this one, I guess
we'll split it out

00:41:19.670 --> 00:41:21.860
by line instead of stacked.

00:41:21.860 --> 00:41:24.170
So that way we can see
sort of the variability

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:25.520
across different nodes.

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:28.370
And another thing is that since
we created this dashboard based

00:41:28.370 --> 00:41:31.582
on this tag that's going
to apply to all the VMs,

00:41:31.582 --> 00:41:33.290
as we add more VMs,
it will automatically

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:35.277
become part of this chart.

00:41:35.277 --> 00:41:36.860
As we have more
clusters, it will also

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:38.318
become part of this
chart, if we're

00:41:38.318 --> 00:41:42.260
targeting those other clusters
with the same group tag.

00:41:42.260 --> 00:41:45.610
So let's see, what else is fun--

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:46.780
memory used.

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:50.580
Memory used is likely
something that you'd

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:54.400
want to set maybe an alert on
or something like this to see--

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:57.150
or when debugging a job, this
is where you can potentially

00:41:57.150 --> 00:41:57.900
look at some skew.

00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:01.667
So just by hovering over it,
you can see what kind of skew

00:42:01.667 --> 00:42:02.500
you're dealing with.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.815
So some workers might be using
a lot of memory and OOMing.

00:42:05.815 --> 00:42:08.440
And you'll find that you need to
chop up your data differently.

00:42:08.440 --> 00:42:11.515
So this is also a development
tool for your pipelines.

00:42:11.515 --> 00:42:13.729
I should have come
back here earlier.

00:42:13.729 --> 00:42:14.770
We missed them coming in.

00:42:14.770 --> 00:42:18.155
But sure enough, we have
a hundred active nodes.

00:42:18.155 --> 00:42:19.030
So it was that quick.

00:42:19.030 --> 00:42:20.620
And actually it comes
in faster than it

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:21.703
takes to deploy a cluster.

00:42:21.703 --> 00:42:22.390
We resize it up.

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:25.780
In less than a minute, we
should have our extra capacity.

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:28.630
And also sure enough,
this whole thing sped up.

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:31.900
Should have seen it in
action, but the second we

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:33.880
added all those nodes,
this thing sped up.

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:35.980
And suddenly instead
of waiting 30 minutes,

00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:38.820
it's done within the span
of basically our demo.

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:46.900
So let's see, once you're done--

00:42:46.900 --> 00:42:49.820
so right, I also created
a Hadoop 2 cluster.

00:42:49.820 --> 00:42:52.030
So just for fun, let's
take a peek at that.

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:56.860
I think I called that
3, Hadoop 3, or demo-3.

00:42:56.860 --> 00:43:00.970
So here is the shiny new
Hadoop 2.8 UI, subtly

00:43:00.970 --> 00:43:02.680
different from Hadoop 2.7.

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:04.510
But if you click
on About, you can

00:43:04.510 --> 00:43:08.830
see this is indeed built
from a Hadoop 2.8 snapshot.

00:43:08.830 --> 00:43:12.760
So this cluster will be running
Hadoop 2.8 with Spark 2.1.

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:15.160
And in general, this is
a great way to canary.

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:19.964
If you're doing development,
a fast development cycle,

00:43:19.964 --> 00:43:21.380
clusters are so
throw away anyway.

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:23.020
If something goes
wrong, throw it away

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.270
and you only last a
couple of minutes.

00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:28.600
So generally, you can
use this preview image

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:31.870
to stay abreast of all the
developments in the open source

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:35.680
ecosystem and have very agile
development against the latest

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:37.670
and greatest.

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:40.490
So we can wait for
that to finish.

00:43:40.490 --> 00:43:42.640
Once you're done
with your clusters,

00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:44.590
it's just as easy to
delete your clusters

00:43:44.590 --> 00:43:46.130
as it was to create them.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:50.286
You can click some
checkboxes, click Delete,

00:43:50.286 --> 00:43:52.160
delete multiple clusters
at once if you want.

00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:59.290
And we're just sorting data.

00:43:59.290 --> 00:44:02.680
We don't have to
watch it finish.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:05.330
So, yeah, it's that easy.

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:06.540
So that about summarizes it.

00:44:06.540 --> 00:44:11.390
Let's go back to the slides
and we can recap what we saw.

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:14.720
So again, to recap
where we saw, Dataproc

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:17.770
is built on these
web-based REST APIs that

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:19.870
allow consistent and
ubiquitous access

00:44:19.870 --> 00:44:23.450
from a variety of clients,
both from Google-provided tools

00:44:23.450 --> 00:44:26.330
like that gcloud command
line and Cloud Console,

00:44:26.330 --> 00:44:28.370
as well as third
parties for integration.

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:32.000
So there's a thriving ecosystem
here of things like Airflow

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:35.300
adding orchestration first
class for Dataproc and Luigi

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:37.370
and Python mrjob
that all integrate

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:39.980
with these web-based APIs.

00:44:39.980 --> 00:44:43.190
We also saw how job submission
preserves the look and feel,

00:44:43.190 --> 00:44:45.890
whether you're using low level
clients or gcloud dataproc job

00:44:45.890 --> 00:44:47.060
submit.

00:44:47.060 --> 00:44:48.860
But by submitting
through the Jobs API,

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:51.590
you get this value
add of logging,

00:44:51.590 --> 00:44:54.320
of job tracking,
of status tracking,

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:57.850
of viewing your job driver
output from the Cloud Console,

00:44:57.850 --> 00:45:00.762
and more to come as well.

00:45:04.690 --> 00:45:08.784
We also saw that it's trivially
easy to update your cluster,

00:45:08.784 --> 00:45:09.950
even in the middle of a job.

00:45:09.950 --> 00:45:14.610
So if you underestimated
how much resources your job

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:17.340
would take and you want to
get it done fast, no big deal.

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:18.480
No harm done.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:21.180
Click a couple of buttons
and you're good to go again.

00:45:21.180 --> 00:45:23.350
You can speed up that job.

00:45:23.350 --> 00:45:26.010
And finally we saw
integration with Stackdriver,

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:28.530
both on the logging side where
you can see your user logs,

00:45:28.530 --> 00:45:30.990
you can see your daemon logs,
and on the monitoring side

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:34.680
where it's easy to
just point and click

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:36.360
in order to create
customized monitoring

00:45:36.360 --> 00:45:40.065
dashboards in the same place
across different GCP services,

00:45:40.065 --> 00:45:42.390
so that you can monitor
everything, track everything,

00:45:42.390 --> 00:45:45.870
set up alerts all
through Stackdriver.

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:48.510
So let's recap
what it looks like

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:51.330
to put together everything
we learned together,

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:53.310
just to illustrate
what this might

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:55.530
look like at various
stages of your migration.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:58.649
So to begin with, you might have
some amount of archival data.

00:45:58.649 --> 00:46:00.690
You might be looking to
free up space on-premise.

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:04.110
So you're trying to
archive some data to GCS.

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:08.370
Maybe you're trying to set up
disaster recovery, so backing

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.406
up your data to GCS already.

00:46:10.406 --> 00:46:12.030
In such a case it
becomes very natural,

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:15.240
even if you're not forcing
any kind of compute migration,

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:17.670
to start adopting
Dataproc ad-hoc.

00:46:17.670 --> 00:46:20.160
So oftentimes what
happens is some kind

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.440
of unexpected backfill
that you otherwise

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:25.540
wouldn't be able to accommodate
on-premise, no big deal.

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:27.400
You can run it and
you can run it quickly

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:29.600
on Dataproc bursting into cloud.

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:32.670
New development-- why
constrain yourself

00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:35.130
to working on your
development cycle

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:39.404
according to whatever scraps
are available on-premise when

00:46:39.404 --> 00:46:41.070
instead you can sort
of burst into cloud

00:46:41.070 --> 00:46:45.930
and feel free to break things
as much as you want and not

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:49.990
worry about production
as you're doing it?

00:46:49.990 --> 00:46:52.600
In the interim, you might
find yourself with somewhat

00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:53.830
of a hybrid cloud setup.

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:58.600
So by using a VPN tunnel to
bridge your on-premise network

00:46:58.600 --> 00:47:01.750
with a GCE network,
Dataproc natively

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.360
supports deploying onto
these same subnetworks

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.999
so that they can talk natively
to your on-premise clusters.

00:47:06.999 --> 00:47:08.790
And they might be
pulling some of the data.

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:12.250
They might be helping mediate
some of your data transfer.

00:47:12.250 --> 00:47:15.357
They might also be doing
ETL or analytics directly

00:47:15.357 --> 00:47:16.690
against your on-premise cluster.

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:19.540
So either way they're augmenting
your on-premise cluster.

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:21.700
And in the meantime
on the other side,

00:47:21.700 --> 00:47:24.070
you'll find that you still
have some subset of things

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:30.130
as more of your data starts
making use of GCP's storage

00:47:30.130 --> 00:47:30.842
options.

00:47:30.842 --> 00:47:32.800
You'll find that you can
burst your development

00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:33.520
pipeline still.

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:36.310
So same idea as purely
bursting into the cloud,

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:41.260
but these will be augmenting
your overall platform, even

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:43.960
with Dataproc clusters that
aren't directly accessing

00:47:43.960 --> 00:47:45.860
your on-premise cluster.

00:47:45.860 --> 00:47:48.160
And finally we see a
cloud native architecture

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:52.430
where everything is
based on cloud storage.

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:54.610
And again, instead
of any Hadoop cluster

00:47:54.610 --> 00:47:57.640
being your universe of
data analytics tooling,

00:47:57.640 --> 00:48:01.960
GCP as a whole is
your data platform.

00:48:01.960 --> 00:48:04.840
And you'll be able to unify all
of your logging and monitoring

00:48:04.840 --> 00:48:09.250
across different tools in
one place using Stackdriver.

00:48:09.250 --> 00:48:12.010
You might find third
party tools to be

00:48:12.010 --> 00:48:15.300
useful for performing your
scheduling and orchestration

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:18.070
and productionisation
of your Dataproc jobs.

00:48:18.070 --> 00:48:21.250
And you'll likely find some
mixture of completely ephemeral

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:24.030
jobs that are very hands
off, easy to manage,

00:48:24.030 --> 00:48:27.100
while others that might not
have come along so easily

00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:29.982
but still are amenable to
semi-long lived clusters.

00:48:29.982 --> 00:48:31.690
And again, with
semi-long lived clusters,

00:48:31.690 --> 00:48:33.400
you still eliminate
the headaches

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:36.520
of worrying about this
two-year-old cluster where

00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:39.039
you're worried that a gust
of wind might blow it over

00:48:39.039 --> 00:48:40.330
and you don't want to touch it.

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:44.140
Here, you can canary brand
new versions of software.

00:48:44.140 --> 00:48:49.780
And if a new guy comes in and
breaks an entire production

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:50.920
cluster, no big deal.

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:53.740
You throw it away, create a
new one, let it join the group.

00:48:53.740 --> 00:48:59.080
And it's that easy to
manage your infrastructure.

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:01.570
So that pretty much
concludes today's session.

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:04.720
A couple of related sessions
include this afternoon,

00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:09.070
there's going to be BD205, which
is a more higher level view

00:49:09.070 --> 00:49:13.570
of Dataproc with some neat
demos, less migration oriented.

00:49:13.570 --> 00:49:15.310
Machine learning
on Spark MLlib is

00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:16.660
going to be tomorrow morning.

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:18.830
And IO321 was concurrent
with this one,

00:49:18.830 --> 00:49:22.310
but you can catch it
on recorded sessions

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:24.670
if you have the time for a
deeper infrastructure level

00:49:24.670 --> 00:49:27.970
dive into GCS performance.

00:49:27.970 --> 00:49:29.080
Just some doc links--

00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:32.200
you can find a lot of good
reading materials under

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:34.855
cloud.google.com/dataproc.

00:49:34.855 --> 00:49:36.730
You can also see those
initialization actions

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:38.950
I talked about hosted
on GitHub and maintained

00:49:38.950 --> 00:49:44.890
by the Google team, along with a
lot of ecosystem contributions.

00:49:44.890 --> 00:49:47.140
And various sources of help--

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:51.160
naturally you'll have your
Cloud Support contracts

00:49:51.160 --> 00:49:54.760
where you can contact high level
escalations of Cloud Support

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:56.140
troubleshooting.

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:58.750
There's also a very active
StackOverflow community.

00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:02.440
And of course under our normal
cloud.google.com documentation,

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:06.000
you can find a lot
of helpful links.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:06.940
So thanks.

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:10.290
